 and thematic elements present in 'The Last of the Mohicans,' influencing Cooper's narrative approach."}], "places": [{"name": "Cooperstown, USA", "role": "Birthplace"}, {"name": "New York, USA", "role": "Setting"}, {"name": "Lake George, USA", "role": "Major battle"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: The Last of the Mohicans", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "the-last-of-the-mohicans"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q493574", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Last_of_the_Mohicans"], "tags": ["literary work", "Novels & Prose Fiction", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "The Last of the Mohicans contributed to the historical record of the Modern period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The novel shaped American perceptions of Native Americans and influenced the development of historical fiction, leading to ongoing discussions about representation and colonialism.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-11T13:58:15Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27351656017", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "1826 novel by james fenimore cooper Created by James Fenimore Cooper. Created c. 1826.", "newValue": "Published in 1826, "The Last of the Mohicans" is a historical novel by James Fenimore Cooper that plays a crucial role in American literature and the portrayal of Native American cultures. Set against the backdrop of the French and Indian War (1754-1763), the narrative unfolds in the lush wilderness of the American frontier, particularly focusing on the interactions between Native Americans and colonial settlers. The novel is significant for its vivid depiction of the Mohican tribe, represented by characters like Uncas and Chingachgook, and for its commentary on the often tragic consequences of colonial expansion in North America.

The plot centers around the plight of the last surviving members of the Mohican tribe as they navigate the dangers posed by both British and French forces, as well as rival Native American tribes. Key events include the harrowing journey of the protagonists—Hawkeye, Uncas, and Chingachgook—as they attempt to rescue the daughters of a British colonel, Alice and Cora Munro, amidst the chaos of war. The novel not only serves as a thrilling adventure but also critiques the imperial aspirations of European powers, highlighting the tragic fate of indigenous peoples caught in their conflicts.

